window.__labLoaded({"id":"8.4","title":"Multiple Regression: Many Predictors at Once","goal":"Predict house price from square feet and bedrooms together, and see why the bedrooms effect disappears once size is held fixed, using the lesson's ten houses.","question":"A real-estate agent has ten houses with their size (square feet), number of bedrooms and sale price (in thousands of dollars). A client asks: if I add a bedroom, how much more is the house worth? Bigger houses have more bedrooms and higher prices, so is the bedroom doing the work, or is it just standing in for size?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the ten houses","text":"Square feet, bedrooms and price (in thousands of dollars). Bigger houses tend to have more bedrooms, so the two predictors are tangled together. That is the whole point of this lab.","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'sqft': [1000, 1200, 1400, 1600, 1800, 2000, 2200, 2400, 2600, 2800],\n    'bedrooms': [2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 6],\n    'price': [250, 280, 310, 350, 390, 420, 450, 480, 520, 550]})\n\nprint(df.corr().round(3))","explain":["Load pandas, which holds data in tables (DataFrames).","Load statsmodels' formula interface, which lets us write models like 'price ~ sqft'.","Build a table from a dictionary: each name becomes a column and each list holds that column's ten values.","Square feet of each house, from 1,000 to 2,800.","Number of bedrooms in each house, in the same order.","Sale price in thousands of dollars, in the same order.","Print the correlation of every pair of columns, rounded to 3 decimals, to see how tangled the variables are."],"meaning":"Price is almost perfectly linked to size (0.999) and strongly to bedrooms (0.975). But size and bedrooms are also strongly linked to each other (0.976): bigger houses simply have more bedrooms. When predictors are tangled like this, it is hard to say which one is doing the work.","out":"           sqft  bedrooms  price\nsqft      1.000     0.976  0.999\nbedrooms  0.976     1.000  0.975\nprice     0.999     0.975  1.000","figs":[]},{"title":"Bedrooms alone","text":"On its own, bedrooms seems to matter a lot: each extra bedroom goes with a large jump in price. But bedrooms may just be standing in for size.","code":"alone = smf.ols('price ~ bedrooms', data=df).fit()\n\nprint(f'bedrooms alone: {alone.params[\"bedrooms\"]:.1f} thousand per bedroom')\nprint(f'R squared = {alone.rsquared:.3f}')","explain":["Fit a model with bedrooms as the only predictor. 'price ~ bedrooms' reads 'price explained by bedrooms'.","Print its slope: the average price change per extra bedroom if we ignore size.","Print R squared, the share of price variation that bedrooms alone explain."],"meaning":"On its own, bedrooms looks powerful: each extra bedroom goes with 75.6 thousand dollars more, and R squared is 0.952. That is the figure a simple regression would give the client. But it may be size in disguise.","out":"bedrooms alone: 75.6 thousand per bedroom\nR squared = 0.952","figs":[]},{"title":"Both predictors together","text":"Adding sqft to the formula with + fits one model with two predictors. Each coefficient is the change in price for one more unit of that predictor while the other is held fixed.","code":"both = smf.ols('price ~ sqft + bedrooms', data=df).fit()\n\nprint(both.params.round(4))\nprint()\nprint(both.pvalues.round(4))","explain":["Put both predictors in one model. The + in the formula adds sqft next to bedrooms.","Print the intercept and the two coefficients, rounded to 4 decimals.","Print an empty line to separate the two outputs.","Print each coefficient's p-value. It tests whether that coefficient could really be 0, given the other predictor is in the model."],"meaning":"With both in the model, sqft has a coefficient of 0.1668: each extra square foot adds about 0.167 thousand dollars ($167), holding bedrooms fixed. Bedrooms drops to 0.8065 (about $806) with p = 0.8717, far above 0.05. Once size is accounted for, bedrooms adds essentially nothing.","out":"Intercept    80.0645\nsqft          0.1668\nbedrooms      0.8065\ndtype: float64\n\nIntercept    0.0000\nsqft         0.0000\nbedrooms     0.8717\ndtype: float64","figs":[]},{"title":"Read the fit and what 'held fixed' means","text":"The sqft coefficient compares two houses with the same number of bedrooms and different sizes. The bedrooms coefficient compares houses of the same size with different bedrooms. R squared says how much of the price variation the pair explains.","code":"print(f'R squared          = {both.rsquared:.4f}')\nprint(f'adjusted R squared = {both.rsquared_adj:.4f}')\nprint(f'overall F = {both.fvalue:.1f}, p = {both.f_pvalue:.2e}')\n\nnew = pd.DataFrame({'sqft': [1500], 'bedrooms': [3]})\nprint(f'predicted price, 1500 sqft, 3 bed = {both.predict(new)[0]:.1f}')","explain":["R squared for the two-predictor model: how much of the price variation they explain together.","Adjusted R squared, with a small penalty for each predictor so that adding a useless one does not look like progress.","The overall F test asks whether the predictors together explain anything. .2e prints the p-value in scientific notation.","Describe a new house: 1,500 square feet and 3 bedrooms, as a one-row table with the same column names.","Put the new house through the fitted model to predict its price."],"meaning":"Together the two predictors explain 99.87% of the price variation (adjusted 99.83%), and the overall F test is overwhelming (F = 2688.9, p = 7.92e-11). Most of that comes from sqft. The model predicts 332.6 thousand dollars for 1,500 sq ft and 3 bedrooms. These are correlations from ten houses, not proof of what causes price.","out":"R squared          = 0.9987\nadjusted R squared = 0.9983\noverall F = 2688.9, p = 7.92e-11\npredicted price, 1500 sqft, 3 bed = 332.6","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"sqft <- c(1000, 1200, 1400, 1600, 1800, 2000, 2200, 2400, 2600, 2800)\nbedrooms <- c(2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 6)\nprice <- c(250, 280, 310, 350, 390, 420, 450, 480, 520, 550)\nsummary(lm(price ~ bedrooms))\nfit <- lm(price ~ sqft + bedrooms)\nsummary(fit)\npredict(fit, data.frame(sqft = 1500, bedrooms = 3))","expect":"bedrooms alone: slope 75.6; both: Intercept 80.06, sqft 0.1668 (p < 0.0001), bedrooms 0.806 (p-value 0.872); Multiple R-squared 0.9987, Adjusted 0.9983; F-statistic 2689 on 2 and 7 DF, p-value 7.9e-11; prediction 332.6","note":"The coefficients, R-squared and F match Python; R prints very small p-values in scientific notation such as 1e-05.","explain":["Square feet of the ten houses.","Number of bedrooms of the ten houses, in the same order.","Sale price in thousands of dollars, in the same order.","Fit and summarise a model with bedrooms alone. lm(price ~ bedrooms) means 'price explained by bedrooms'.","Fit the model with two predictors; the + adds sqft. We save it as fit so we can reuse it.","Print the full summary: coefficients with p-values, R-squared, adjusted R-squared and the overall F test.","Predict the price of a new house with 1,500 sq ft and 3 bedrooms, supplied as a data frame."],"read":"In the first summary, bedrooms alone has Estimate 75.6. In the second, sqft has Estimate 0.1668 with a tiny p-value, while bedrooms has Estimate 0.806 and Pr(>|t|) 0.872: not significant once size is in. Multiple R-squared is 0.9987 and the F-statistic is 2689. predict gives 332.6."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Regression > Linear (Dependent: price, Independents: sqft and bedrooms)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / sqft bedrooms price.\nBEGIN DATA\n1000 2 250  1200 2 280  1400 3 310  1600 3 350  1800 4 390\n2000 4 420  2200 4 450  2400 5 480  2600 5 520  2800 6 550\nEND DATA.\nREGRESSION\n  /STATISTICS COEFF R ANOVA\n  /DEPENDENT price\n  /METHOD=ENTER sqft bedrooms.","note":"Read B and Sig. in the Coefficients table, R Square and Adjusted R Square in the Model Summary, and F in the ANOVA table.","explain":["Declare three variables to read in free format: sqft, bedrooms and price.","Marks the start of the data typed into the syntax.","The first five houses, each as sqft, bedrooms then price.","The last five houses in the same layout.","Marks the end of the data.","REGRESSION starts a regression model.","Ask for the coefficients, R and the ANOVA table (which holds the overall F test).","The outcome we want to predict is price.","Enter both predictors, sqft and bedrooms, in one model. The full stop ends the command."],"read":"In the Coefficients table, read B and Sig. for each row: sqft B = .167, Sig. = .000; bedrooms B = .806, Sig. = .872 (not significant). Model Summary: R Square = .999, Adjusted R Square = .998. ANOVA: F = 2688.9, Sig. = .000. To see bedrooms alone, run the model again with only bedrooms."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 10\ngenerate sqft = 800 + 200 * _n\ngenerate bedrooms = real(word(\"2 2 3 3 4 4 4 5 5 6\", _n))\ngenerate price = real(word(\"250 280 310 350 390 420 450 480 520 550\", _n))\nregress price bedrooms\nregress price sqft bedrooms\ndisplay _b[_cons] + _b[sqft] * 1500 + _b[bedrooms] * 3","note":"The second regress prints the coefficients, p-values, R-squared, Adj R-squared and the overall F. sqft is built as 800 + 200 x row number, which gives 1000 to 2800 as in the lesson.","explain":["Clear any old data from memory.","Create ten empty rows, one per house.","Make sqft from the row number: 800 + 200 x _n gives 1,000 for house 1 up to 2,800 for house 10, as in the data.","Fill the bedrooms. word() picks the n-th number from the quoted list and real() turns it into a number.","Fill the prices in thousands of dollars the same way.","Regression of price on bedrooms alone, to see the 'ignoring size' effect.","Regression of price on both predictors.","Predict the price of a 1,500 sq ft, 3 bedroom house: intercept + sqft coefficient x 1500 + bedrooms coefficient x 3."],"read":"In the first regress, bedrooms coef is 75.6. In the second, sqft coef .1668 has P>|t| 0.000 while bedrooms coef .8065 has P>|t| 0.872. Also read R-squared 0.9987, Adj R-squared 0.9983 and F(2, 7) = 2688.9. The display shows 332.6, the predicted price."},"conclusion":"Ignoring size, each bedroom seemed to add $75,600. Holding size fixed, a bedroom adds almost nothing (0.8 thousand dollars, p = 0.87), while each extra square foot adds about $167, so the bedrooms were mostly standing in for size. Caution: size and bedrooms are so tangled (r = 0.98) that ten houses cannot cleanly separate them, and this shows association, not what a renovation would do.","practice":{"scenario":"A courier company records, for ten deliveries, the distance driven (km), the number of stops and the total delivery time in minutes. Longer trips usually have more stops. How much time does each extra km add, and each extra stop, when the other is held fixed?","data":"distance = [2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12, 13, 15, 18]\nstops = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 7]\nminutes = [14, 22, 25, 33, 36, 44, 50, 56, 66, 80]","tasks":["Check the correlations between the three variables.","Fit minutes on stops alone and note the coefficient.","Fit minutes on distance and stops together; report both coefficients, their p-values and R squared.","Predict the time for 10 km and 4 stops, and explain how the stops coefficient changed."],"hint":"Build a DataFrame and use smf.ols('minutes ~ distance + stops', data=df).fit(). Compare the stops coefficient with and without distance in the model.","solution":{"code":"import pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'distance': [2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12, 13, 15, 18],\n                   'stops': [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 7],\n                   'minutes': [14, 22, 25, 33, 36, 44, 50, 56, 66, 80]})\nprint(df.corr().round(3))\nprint(smf.ols('minutes ~ stops', data=df).fit().params.round(2).to_dict())\nm = smf.ols('minutes ~ distance + stops', data=df).fit()\nprint(m.params.round(3).to_dict())\nprint(m.pvalues.round(4).to_dict())\nprint(f'R squared = {m.rsquared:.4f}, adjusted = {m.rsquared_adj:.4f}')\nprint(m.predict(pd.DataFrame({'distance': [10], 'stops': [4]})).round(1).tolist())","answer":"Distance and stops are tightly linked (0.990). Stops alone give 10.93 minutes per stop. With both in the model, each km adds 2.352 minutes (p = 0.0029) and each stop adds 4.613 minutes (p = 0.0143), holding the other fixed. The stops effect shrank from 10.93 to 4.61 because stops were partly standing in for distance. R squared = 0.9975 (adjusted 0.9968). A 10 km, 4 stop delivery is predicted at 45.4 minutes.","out":"          distance  stops  minutes\ndistance     1.000  0.990    0.997\nstops        0.990  1.000    0.995\nminutes      0.997  0.995    1.000\n{'Intercept': 2.17, 'stops': 10.93}\n{'Intercept': 3.425, 'distance': 2.352, 'stops': 4.613}\n{'Intercept': 0.0063, 'distance': 0.0029, 'stops': 0.0143}\nR squared = 0.9975, adjusted = 0.9968\n[45.4]","figs":[]}}});
